  "name": "Bugz in the Attic",
  "editorial_article": "Formed in West London in 1996, Bugz in the Attic became a central pillar of the broken beat movement, operating out of their own BitaSuite studio. The collective—which included Orin Walters, Paul Dolby, Kaidi Tatham, Daz-I-Kue, Alex Phountzi, Cliff Scott, Mark Force, and Matt Lord—managed the BitaSweet label and hosted the influential Co-Op club night at Plastic People in Shoreditch. Their sound defined a specific intersection of jazz and electronic rhythms, achieving commercial success when their single 'Booty La La' reached number 44 on the UK Singles Chart in 2005. The group also left a mark on international collaborations, contributing 'Zombie (Part One)' to the Fela Kuti tribute album 'Red Hot and Riot' for the Red Hot Organization. Their first studio album, 'Back in the Doghouse', arrived in 2006 via V2 Records. Following years of output including remix collections like 'Got the Bug', the collective evolved into a smaller unit centered on Afronaut and Mark Force.",
